# ==============================================================================
# CodeConsist Environment Configuration
#
# Quick Setup:
#   1. Copy this file to create your local .env:
#        cp .env.example .env
#   2. Fill in the keys you need below (especially GEMINI_API_KEY and ADMIN credentials).
#   3. Run: npm install && npm run dev
#
# Note:
#   - Secrets are read ONLY by the backend API server (server/).
#   - NEVER prefix private secrets with VITE_ (Vite bundles VITE_* into client JS).
# ==============================================================================

# ==============================================================================
# 1. Google Gemini AI (Recommended for Admin Console)
# ==============================================================================
# Powers the AI Question Assistant in the Admin Console (/admin/challenges).
# It can generate challenges from descriptions, suggest stages, and check for duplicates.
#
# Get a free API key from Google AI Studio:
#   👉 https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
#
# Leave empty if you do not plan to use AI question generation.
GEMINI_API_KEY=your-gemini-api-key-here
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash


# ==============================================================================
# 2. Administrator Account Bootstrap (Recommended)
# ==============================================================================
# Required to sign into the Administrator Console at /admin/login.
#
# On first server boot, CodeConsist reads these, hashes the password with bcrypt,
# and creates the one admin record in server/data/db.json.
#
# Rules:
#   - ADMIN_USER_ID: 3-32 characters (letters, numbers, hyphens, underscores).
#   - ADMIN_PASSWORD: Minimum 12 characters (avoid common weak passwords).
ADMIN_USER_ID=admin
ADMIN_PASSWORD=ChangeThisPassword123!


# ==============================================================================
# 3. API Server & Networking
# ==============================================================================
# Port for the local Node.js API server (server/). Defaults to 4000.
API_PORT=4000

# Signing key for JWT session tokens (both learner and admin sessions).
# In development, one is auto-generated into server/data/.jwt-secret on first run.
# Provide a long random string here for production deployments.
JWT_SECRET=replace-me-with-a-long-random-string

# Where the Vite frontend dev server proxies /api requests.
# Follows API_PORT automatically if left unset.
# VITE_API_PROXY=http://localhost:4000


# ==============================================================================
# 4. Compiled Languages Engine (Optional: Java, C, C++, Go)
# ==============================================================================
# JavaScript and Python execute locally without any external dependencies:
#   - JavaScript runs in a sandboxed Node child process / Web Worker.
#   - Python runs in WebAssembly via Pyodide.
#
# To execute Java, C, C++, or Go in the playground, configure a Judge0 instance
# (e.g. self-hosted Judge0 or via RapidAPI's judge0-ce):
#
# JUDGE0_API_URL=https://judge0-ce.p.rapidapi.com
# JUDGE0_API_KEY=your_rapidapi_key
# JUDGE0_API_HOST=judge0-ce.p.rapidapi.com


# ==============================================================================
# 5. Payments via Razorpay (Optional)
# ==============================================================================
# --- Optional: payments (Razorpay) ---
# One-time purchases: the lifetime licence, a whole track, a single premium
# stage, or a verified certificate. Prices are set in the admin console at
# /admin/billing (defaults are built in) - nothing here decides a price.
#
# Leave all three unset and the server runs in TEST MODE: checkout completes
# through an explicit "Complete test payment" step, clearly labelled
# "Test mode - no money moves". Nothing is ever charged and nothing unlocks
# silently.
#
# To take real payments, create a key pair in the Razorpay dashboard
# (Settings -> API Keys) and add a webhook pointing at
#   https://<your-domain>/api/billing/webhook
# subscribed to the "payment.captured" and "order.paid" events, with the
# secret you choose there in RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET.
#
# Only RAZORPAY_KEY_ID ever reaches the browser (the checkout script needs
# it). The key secret and the webhook secret stay on the server, are used
# only to sign and verify, and are never logged or returned.
#
# RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=rzp_test_xxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=your-razorpay-key-secret
# RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=your-webhook-secret


# ==============================================================================
# 6. Optional: sign in with Google / GitHub
# ==============================================================================
# --- Optional: sign in with Google / GitHub ---
# Adds "Continue with Google" and "Continue with GitHub" above the email and
# password form. Email + password keeps working exactly as before either way -
# it is the fallback, not the thing being replaced.
#
# Leave these unset and nothing changes: the server reports that no provider
# is configured, the browser shows no buttons, and there is nothing to break.
#
# Google - https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
#   Create Credentials -> OAuth client ID -> Web application, then add this
#   EXACT Authorised redirect URI (note the API port, 4000, not the web app's
#   3000 - the browser is sent back to the API server, which then hands the
#   app a session):
#     http://localhost:4000/api/auth/oauth/google/callback
#
# GitHub - https://github.com/settings/developers
#   New OAuth App, with this EXACT Authorization callback URL:
#     http://localhost:4000/api/auth/oauth/github/callback
#
# The rule behind those two URLs: the server sends the browser back to
# "<origin>/api/auth/oauth/<provider>/callback", where <origin> is APP_ORIGIN
# if it is set and otherwise the address the request arrived on. Whatever that
# origin is, register the matching URL - it has to agree character for
# character, or the provider refuses the exchange.
#
#   - Running `npm run dev` (web on :3000, API on :4000) and want to land back
#     on the dev server after signing in? Set APP_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000
#     and register http://localhost:3000/api/auth/oauth/<provider>/callback
#     instead - :3000 proxies /api to the API, so both halves still work.
#   - Running `npm start` (one process serving the built app and the API),
#     leave APP_ORIGIN unset locally and keep the :4000 URLs above.
#   - In production, set APP_ORIGIN to the public origin of the site and
#     register "<APP_ORIGIN>/api/auth/oauth/<provider>/callback".
#
# These four values are read ONLY by the API server (server/oauth.js). None of
# them is ever bundled into the browser, returned by a route or written to a
# log - the client is told nothing but "google: true / false".
#
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-google-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-google-client-secret
# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=your-github-client-id
# GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=your-github-client-secret

# Where the browser is sent back to after a provider sign-in. Unset is right
# for local development (the server uses the address the request came in on).
# APP_ORIGIN=https://your-domain.example


# ==============================================================================
# 7. Microsoft Excel Sync via Microsoft Graph API (Optional)
# ==============================================================================
# Mirrors learner signups, logins, and progress into an Excel spreadsheet table.
#
# Setup:
#   1. In Azure AD, register an app and grant `Files.ReadWrite.All` under Microsoft Graph.
#   2. Create a table in OneDrive/SharePoint with columns:
#      User ID | Username | Email | Signup Date | Last Active | XP | Level | Streak | Stages Completed | Challenges Completed | Is Premium
#
# MICROSOFT_TENANT_ID=your-azure-ad-tenant-id
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=your-app-registration-client-id
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret-value
# MICROSOFT_EXCEL_DRIVE_ID=the-onedrive-or-sharepoint-drive-id
# MICROSOFT_EXCEL_WORKBOOK_ID=the-workbook-items-drive-item-id
# MICROSOFT_EXCEL_WORKSHEET_NAME=Users
# MICROSOFT_EXCEL_TABLE_NAME=Users
